elf only when it is the implementer actor. Use the returned packet only when `ready` is true. If existing independent reports reveal a possible recurring failure, follow [recurring failure guidance](../../../.joycraft/references/recurring-failures.md) before carrying it forward. Before the first dispatch, claim the queue with your actual host session ID and pass it as `--coordinator-session` to every startup: ```sh node .joycraft/runtime/coordinator.mjs claim --queue <queue> --session <your-session-id> --host <host> ``` If another session holds the queue, stop and report the holder; use `--take-over` only when the user says that session has ended. Then run `node .joycraft/runtime/queue-check.mjs --queue <queue>`; act on each gap. For a spec marked `execution.parallel: true`, give each concurrent mutator its own tree and build/cache state, on every host: `node .joycraft/runtime/worktrees.mjs create --queue <queue> --spec <id> --session <your-session-id>`. Launch its worker without host worktree isolation (Claude Code: no Agent `isolation`), naming the printed `workerRoot`. It first runs the project bootstrap. Read [worker isolation](../../../.joycraft/references/worktrees.md) when parallel work, integration, cleanup, or resource capacity is in play. If isolation cannot be obtained, replan the affected wave and set the current queue entry's `execution.parallel` to `false` before dispatch, recording why; then run it serially. Otherwise leave it unstarted. Implement the spec; run checks and any relevant rendered inspection as you go, in the tiers of [running tests](../../../.joycraft/references/testing.md#running-tests). Tests come from the spec, tagged `[AC:<criterion-id>]`. Add a test only when it can fail for a reason no existing test can; one test may carry several `[AC:]` tags and cover several checks; extend the existing test file first. A new acceptance test runs once and fails before the code exists; its receipt comes first. Do not commit tests of one-off run st