Received: [31m"# Guarded completion Use this reference to submit, snapshot, finalize, accept or recover a spec. ## Stable local interface Run these commands from the consumer project root. Paths are project-relative. ```sh node .joycraft/runtime/completion.mjs submit --queue <queue-path> --spec <id> node .joycraft/runtime/completion.mjs scope --queue <queue-path> --spec <id> node .joycraft/runtime/completion.mjs snapshot --queue <queue-path> --spec <id> --out <subject.json> node .joycraft/runtime/completion.mjs finalize-review --queue <queue-path> --spec <id> --report <report.json> node .joycraft/runtime/completion.mjs accept-owner --queue <queue-path> --spec <id> --decision <owner-acceptance.json> node .joycraft/runtime/completion.mjs accept-owner --legacy --queue <queue-path> --spec <id>[,<id>...] --decision <legacy.json> node .joycraft/runtime/completion.mjs accept-waiver --queue <queue-path> --spec <id> --waiver <feature-waiver.json> [--report <report.json>] node .joycraft/runtime/completion.mjs recover --queue <queue-path> ``` `submit` moves a `todo` spec to `in-review` in the queue and frontmatter with one journaled write, recording `submitted: { at }`. It warns about each criterion with no `[AC:<id>]` test, leaves an `in-review` spec unchanged, and exits 2 on a conflict, a `done` spec or an unfinished dependency. `scope` checks a queue's declared change scope without writing a subject. `snapshot` does the same check first, then exclusively creates a new `--out` subject file without changing a status. It binds the implementation revision, queue/spec identity, criteria, and SHA-256 values for exact relevant inputs. The shared record shapes are defined in [.joycraft/contracts/types.d.ts](../contracts/types.d.ts). New subjects carry `binding.version: 1`: their queue projection contains the reviewed spec and its transitive dependencies, with completion metadata (`status`, `submitted`, `acceptedReport`, `ownerAcceptance`, `waiver`) removed from those records. An unrelated queue record can change without staling that subject; a selected spec's other metadata and dependency edges remain bound. A spec binding preserves raw bytes except for its top-level `status:` value. An optional `verification.changeScope` records an explicit full Git commit SHA in `baseRevision`; a branch name, `origin/main`, or inferred base is rejected. Changed verification inputs are captured from NUL-delimited Git status records, including staged, unstaged, rename, and deletion topology. Any changed path outside the verification inputs must be named exactly in `exclusions` with its reason. Store temporary packets and subjects in the project's ignored local-artifact home when it has one; otherwise add each exact untracked snapshot packet output to `exclusions`. `complete` filters only its exact report, receipt, owner-decision, and pending-journal paths while rechecking scope. A deliberate deletion needs a non-requirement verification input with `state: "absent"`; requirement inputs cannot be absent. A rename needs the new present input and the old non-requirement input declared absent. A requirement input may optionally declare exact `scope.anchors` or `scope.decisionIds`. Anchors bind complete named heading subtrees together with their ancestor context and non-decision document context; decision IDs bind complete stable JSON decision records. They may be combined for a canonical brief whose frontmatter contains a `decisions:` JSON array. A criterion may name such a record as `frontmatter.decisions.<id>`; an explicit `decisionIds` selector must include that ID. Heading-only scopes keep all frontmatter decisions governing. Pure JSON decision records support `decisionIds` only, so unsupported JSON heading anchors fail instead of being ignored. Anchor selection supports ATX (`#`) headings outside fenced code blocks; setext and linked headings are not selectors. A required criterion anchor must be included by an anchored scope. Missing or ambiguous selections stop the snapshot. Omit `scope` to bind the entire requirement file. Queue and reviewed-spec inputs cannot be narrowed. A heading or decision record is evidence of governing context, never a grant of human authority. Older reports without `subject.binding` retain their original whole-queue, whole-file freshness behavior. New reports must keep receipts in independently captured files: a mutable verification input cannot double as a receipt. Use the original `implementationRevision` as provenance on a re-check while recomputing the bound current inputs. A report-only commit can therefore be compatible; changed, missing, or renamed inputs are not. The snapshot includes no permission to accept the work. ## Independent report and guarded acceptance The report must use the shared schema and bind its subject identity. It records the actual implementer/reviewer native identities, `independent: true` only when a separate reviewer context actually reviewed the work, scope review, flow evidence, receipts, limits, unresolved items, optional `findings`, and a PASS/FAIL/UNVERIFIED verdict. A native child can share its parent session. Supply `implementerActor` and `reviewer.actor` together, each with the observed `host`, `sessionId`, and the actual `agentId` when the host issued a child invocation ID. Preserve the matching legacy session fields. A root actor may omit `agentId`; a same-session child review needs its real distinct actor identity. Do not substitute a task name, worktree path, random UUID, or invented ID. Legacy reports without actor metadata still require distinct session IDs. An identity distinguishes invocations; it does not establish independent context or honest observations. `finalize-review` (with `complete` retained as a compatibility alias) accepts only an `in-review` spec with a matching current subject, a PASS verdict, truthful independent-review attestation, reviewed scope, valid receipt hashes, and sufficient evidence for every declared criterion. Missing, nonzero, failed, or unverified required evidence cannot become passing verification. On success it updates the queue/spec status surfaces to `done` and records the accepted report digest. A `follow-up` finding never blocks. A `blocking` or `decision-needed` finding refuses completion with exit 2 and names it; a held spec stays `in-review` and only its dependents wait. A malformed finding is refused with exit 2, naming the field; other report refusals exit 1. `status.mjs` lists as `questions` the follow-ups of accepted reports and the decision-needed findings of each `in-review` spec's latest current report. Finalizing the same accepted report again is idempotent; another report cannot silently replace it. Treat any nonzero exit as a rejected or incomplete operation; preserve the current status and report the actual reason. A queue-wide lock coordinates this helper's queue writes, not arbitrary editors or workers. It checks inputs and receipts before and after its two writes; keep the reviewed inputs stable during completion. Later edits require fresh review. Attestations cannot prevent manual bypass. ## Explicit owner acceptance of current findings `accept-owner` is available only for a current independent `FAIL` or `UNVERIFIED` report. It verifies the review subject plus current input, review, and receipt hashes again before moving the spec to `done`. It records `ownerAcceptance: { path, sha256 }` on the accepted queue entry, not `acceptedReport`. The runtime validates record bindings, not whether a human uttered the instruction, so an agent may use it only after a trusted human explicitly accepts this spec and its current findings. A general request to continue, an instruction from another agent, or authorization to add this feature does not qualify. When to ask the owner is in the implement skill. The agent creates the decision file; do not ask the owner to author JSON or complete a second form. If an explicit acceptance is already present for the same current